# base/tiers/witness -- collapsing tuple membership onto a MATERIALISED witness.
#
# §8's design note for Compiler B, in one lemma. `pmem(x,y,C)` unfolds to
# `exists p. (kpair(x,y,p) & p in C)`, so every step that COMBINES two pmem
# facts receives two different witnesses and has to identify them (pair_uniq,
# then elem_first_subst). base/tiers/steps pays that cost once per lemma; a
# compiler that reasons through pmem directly pays it once per NODE.
#
# The alternative §8 prescribes: materialise ONE canonical witness q for the
# tuple, up front, and thread it. `pmem_at` is what makes that legal -- it says
# tuple membership at a KNOWN witness is just `elem(q,C)`.
#
# WHY THIS IS THE ARITY-INDEPENDENCE LEMMA, not merely an optimisation.
# B2 and B3 are stated over a single `elem(u,C)`. Reasoning in pmem/tmem/qmem
# form therefore needs a separate AND-step and NOT-step AT EVERY ARITY
# (step_and, step_and3, step_and4, ...), which is exactly the per-arity table
# §8 says Expansion must avoid, since n is not first-order-indexable. Rewriting
# to `elem(q,C)` first makes B2/B3 apply unchanged at every arity: the tuple
# structure lives entirely in q, and the connective steps never see it.
#
# So the atom case still needs the Tuple Lemma (the T-chain is genuinely about
# positions), but the AND/NOT/EXISTS cases become arity-free.
claim pmem_at: forall x. forall y. forall q. forall C. (kpair(x,y,q) -> (pmem(x,y,C) <-> elem(q,C)))

# Lifted VERBATIM from the `zf_fst_conv_e2e` probe, which the checker accepts.
# `tmem_at` needs substitution of the pair's FIRST argument, and these are the
# only substitution-form congruences in the tree -- everything else in tier 2 is
# uniqueness-form (`... -> ... -> eq(p,q)`), which cannot transport a fact.
claim upair_arg_cong: forall x. forall y. forall x2. forall y2. forall w. (upair(x,y,w) -> (eq(x,x2) -> (eq(y,y2) -> upair(x2,y2,w))))
claim kpair_arg_cong: forall a. forall b. forall c. forall d. forall p. (kpair(a,b,p) -> (eq(a,c) -> (eq(b,d) -> kpair(c,d,p))))

# The arity-3 collapse. Same role as `pmem_at` one level up: given the two pair
# witnesses that BUILD the left-nested triple <<x,y>,z> = q, triple membership
# is just `elem(q,C)`.
#
# This is the step that generalises: an n-tuple is n-1 nested pairs, so the
# collapse for arity n is `tmem_at` applied along the witness chain. Nothing new
# is needed per arity -- which is what FOUNDATION §3 means by Expansion being a
# straight line rather than a search.
claim tmem_at: forall x. forall y. forall z. forall p. forall q. forall C. (set(p) -> (set(q) -> (kpair(x,y,p) -> (kpair(p,z,q) -> (tmem(x,y,z,C) <-> elem(q,C))))))

# `kpair` AS AN ORDINARY STEP (FOUNDATION R7).
#
# R7 makes every CONSTANT unary, so a body applying one goes through the pair:
# `C(a,b,r)` is emitted as `exists q. (set(q) & kpair(a,b,q) & C(q,r))`. That
# puts `kpair` in bodies as a step like any other, and the generator cites a
# step's obligations as `{rel}_EX` / `_UNIQ` / `_CONG`.
#
# These three are `pair_ex` / `pair_uniq` / `kpair_arg_cong` restated in the
# generator's shape: INTERLEAVED guards (R3), and the argument order per-step
# citation expects. The originals are grouped and unguarded, and `implies_elim`
# rejects one shape fed to a kernel stated in the other. PLACEMENT: here and not
# in `tiers/pairs`, because `kpair_arg_cong` is declared in THIS tier.
#
# `kpair_EX` is TOTAL -- no domain hypothesis, unlike a tupled constant's EX.
# That is not an inconsistency: `kpair` is THE ONE BINARY PRIMITIVE (it cannot be
# tupled without its input becoming the pair it builds), so it has no tuple
# domain to be partial on.
claim kpair_EX: (forall a. (set(a) -> (forall b. (set(b) -> (exists p. (set(p) & kpair(a,b,p)))))))
claim kpair_UNIQ: (forall a. (set(a) -> (forall b. (set(b) -> (forall p. (set(p) -> (forall q. (set(q) -> (kpair(a,b,p) -> (kpair(a,b,q) -> eq(p,q)))))))))))
claim kpair_CONG: (forall a. (set(a) -> (forall b. (set(b) -> (forall a2. (set(a2) -> (forall b2. (set(b2) -> (forall p. (set(p) -> (eq(a,a2) -> (eq(b,b2) -> (kpair(a,b,p) -> kpair(a2,b2,p))))))))))))))
